69, 10242, 15017, +more)]\n\n## Relationship between obesity and hiperuricemia\n\nObesity is closely linked to hiperuricemia, as individuals with obesity are more likely to experience elevated uric acid levels. This relationship is attributed to various metabolic factors that impair the renal elimination of uric acid, thereby exacerbating the condition. Managing obesity is crucial for mitigating the risk of developing hiperuricemia, highlighting the need for integrated health strategies that address both issues simultaneously. [Data: Relationships (5902)]\n\n## Hiperuricemia's role in kidney stone formation\n\nHiperuricemia is recognized as a contributing factor to the development of kidney stones, particularly those composed of uric acid. The direct relationship between elevated uric acid levels and the formation of kidney stones emphasizes the importance of monitoring and managing uric acid levels to prevent this painful condition. This association is critical for healthcare providers in advising patients at risk of urolithiasis. [Data: Relationships (15020)]\n\n## Impact of diabetes on hiperuricemia\n\nDiabetes mellitus, particularly type 2 diabetes, is associated with hiperuricemia, as both conditions can coexist and influence each other. The interplay between these two health issues complicates management strategies, as elevated uric acid levels can exacerbate diabetic complications. Understanding this relationship is essential for healthcare providers to develop comprehensive treatment plans for affected patients. [Data: Relationships (1646, 13256)]\n\n## Cardiovascular risks linked to hiperuricemia\n\nHiperuricemia is associated with an increased risk of cardiovascular diseases, particularly in patients with pre-existing conditions. The connection between elevated uric acid levels and cardiovascular health underscores the need for regular monitoring and management of uric acid levels in at-risk populations. This relationship highlights the broader implications of hiperuricemia beyond its immediate health effects. [Data: Relationships (10242)]\n\n## Influence of dietary factors on hiperuricemia\n\nDietary factors play a significant role in the management of hiperuricemia. Increased intake of purines, found in certain foods, can elevate uric acid levels, while a diet rich in vegetables and low-fat dairy products may help lower these levels. Understanding the dietary influences on hiperuricemia is crucial for developing effective dietary recommendations for patients. [Data: Relationships (15030, 15032)]\"\r\n\n\n-----Entities-----\nhuman_readable_id,title,description,degree\r\n3187,ALCOHOL,\"Alcohol is a substance that can have a wide range of effects on health, particularly when consumed in excess. It interacts with various medications, leading to pharmacokinetic and pharmacodynamic interactions that can modify drug metabolism and enhance or diminish their effects. This can result in significant health implications, especially for individuals on medication.\n\nIn terms of metabolic effects, alcohol consumption can influence triglyceride levels in the blood, impacting lipid metabolism. It also has implications for blood sugar control and weight management in diabetic patients, with recommendations for moderate intake. Furthermore, alcohol is a frequent cause of acute pancreatitis, particularly in Scandinavian countries and urban areas of the United States, contributing to up to 75% of cases in some regions.\n\nDietary guidelines address alcohol consumption, recommending moderation to reduce health risks. For instance, it is linked to hypertension, with suggestions to limit intake to manage blood pressure effectively. Additionally, alcohol consumption is associated with higher uric acid levels, increasing the risk of developing gout, especially with certain types of alcoholic beverages.\n\nIn specific populations, such as those who have undergone bariatric surgery or individuals with obesity, alcohol consumption is advised 